course: dictionary
lesson: 12992
title: Explicitly: saying it clearly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Explicitly means in a clear and direct way, so there is no doubt about what is meant. «She explicitly told us not to touch it.» She did not hint or drop a clue. She said it plainly, so nobody could misunderstand.
explain: In reading, a fact is explicitly stated when the writer says it outright. If the writer only hints at it, the fact is implied, and you have to work it out. «Ella said, "I am cross with you."» is explicit. «Ella slammed the door» only implies that she is cross.
explain: In maths, «show your working explicitly» means write down every step, not just the answer. The rules at school work the same way: if something is explicitly forbidden, it is clearly written or spoken, so «I didn't know» is no excuse.
fact: Explicitly means clearly and directly, saying exactly what you mean.
fact: Something is explicitly stated when it is written or said outright, in plain words.
fact: Something is implied when it is hinted at, so you have to work it out from clues.
fact: «Explicitly» can also mean openly showing adult content, which is a grown-up meaning you will meet in rules about films and books.
fact: In a test, «explicitly stated» means the answer can be found word for word in the text, not guessed.
exercise: «The sign explicitly says: No dogs allowed.» What does explicitly mean here? | Now and then, not always | *In a clear and direct way, saying exactly what it means | Very quietly, so nobody can hear it | In a hidden way, with clues to guess
exercise: In a reading test, the question asks: which one is explicitly stated? | «Ella left the room early.» | *«Ella said, "I am cross with you."» | «Ella slammed the door, so she might be cross.» | «Ella's face went pink.»
exercise: What does «show your working explicitly» mean in maths? | *Write down every step, not just the answer | Do the sum in your head | Use a red pen only | Write only the final answer
test: «The rules explicitly say no running in the corridor.» What does explicitly tell you? | The rule might be true sometimes | The rule is a secret that nobody mentions | The rule was made a long time ago | *The rule is clearly written, so nobody can say they did not know
test: «Dad explicitly said we could go to the park.» What does this sentence tell you? | *He said it clearly and directly | He said it while he was asleep | He said it, but he was not sure what he meant | He only hinted, and never said it
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to explicitly? | Hopefully | Slowly | *Clearly | Secretly
